00:26:03,720 --> 00:26:14,720
We wanted to better understand hospitals own creative identity and how music could strengthen our quality of life, diversify our economy, attract talent and help shape the city we were becoming.

197
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:20,720
The first audit identified strengths that were already here, but it also revealed opportunities that we had not fully tapped into.

198
00:26:20,720 --> 00:26:26,720
It gave us a roadmap of actionable recommendations that the city and our community partners have steadily worked on advancing.

199
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:37,720
And that work helped lead to the creation of a my role, the Huntsville Music Office, major investments like the Orion Amphitheater and a much more intentional approach to growing our ecosystem.

200
00:26:38,720 --> 00:26:46,720
And so eight years later, we went back to sound diplomacy to ask how far we'd come. And the data tells a pretty remarkable story.

201
00:26:51,720 --> 00:27:01,720
Let's start with the big picture. In 2018, Huntsville's music ecosystem generated approximately one hundred and ninety one point two million in total economic output.

202
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:07,720
And that's adjusted to inflation. Today, that number has grown to two hundred and seventy eight point two million.

203
00:27:07,720 --> 00:27:15,720
That's an increase of roughly forty five percent. And when we talk about the music economy, we're talking about much more than concerts.

204
00:27:15,720 --> 00:27:23,720
It includes artists, venues, festivals, music education, retail, radio, studios and all the businesses that support music activity.

205
00:27:24,720 --> 00:27:31,720
The simplest takeaway from this is that music is now definitely a significant and measurable part of Huntsville's overall economy.

206
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:39,720
Talk about jobs. I think one of the most exciting indicators about this is the employment growth.

207
00:27:39,720 --> 00:27:44,720
The original audit identified nine hundred and eighty six direct music related jobs.

208
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:51,720
Today, Huntsville's music ecosystem supports three thousand forty seven jobs across the broader regional economy.

209
00:27:51,720 --> 00:27:56,720
And that's two thousand four hundred and twenty four jobs directly within the music sector.

210
00:27:56,720 --> 00:28:01,720
That direct workforce is nearly two and a half times the size it was when our work began.

211
00:28:01,720 --> 00:28:05,720
And so that's an increase of about one hundred and forty five percent in the employment sector.

212
00:28:05,720 --> 00:28:13,720
And so that means real employment opportunities not only for artists and performers, but also venue and production professionals, educators,

213
00:28:13,720 --> 00:28:19,720
technicians, promoters and many others. So the ecosystem hasn't simply just gotten busier.

214
00:28:19,720 --> 00:28:23,720
We've grown at larger, deeper and increasingly professionalized.

215
00:28:27,720 --> 00:28:32,720
Another way that economists measure the strength of an industry is through gross value added or GVA.

216
00:28:32,720 --> 00:28:36,720
So essentially that's the value the sector itself contributes to the economy.

217
00:28:36,720 --> 00:28:42,720
Today, Huntsville's music ecosystem contributes approximately one hundred and sixty two million in gross value added.

218
00:28:42,720 --> 00:28:46,720
And this chart shows us where that value is being generated.

219
00:28:46,720 --> 00:28:50,720
Creation is the artists and the songwriters making the music.

220
00:28:50,720 --> 00:28:55,720
Production is the studios, producers and services that turn that creativity into a finished product.

221
00:28:55,720 --> 00:28:59,720
Distribution is how music reaches audiences and markets.

222
00:28:59,720 --> 00:29:04,720
Exhibition is the live side of the industry, such as festivals, venues, concerts and promoters.

223
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:12,720
And transversal is the support system around it all, such as music education, management, equipment, nonprofits and other professional services.

224
00:29:12,720 --> 00:29:19,720
As you can see, exhibition and support services account for the vast majority of the economic value currently being generated.

225
00:29:19,720 --> 00:29:22,720
And so that also reflects something we already know.

226
00:29:22,720 --> 00:29:30,720
Huntsville has become very good at presenting and supporting live music, but it also highlights one of our clearest opportunities for future growth.

227
00:29:30,720 --> 00:29:33,720
And that's the creation and production aspects of things.

228
00:29:33,720 --> 00:29:38,720
And so the next phase of our work is not just going to be about bringing more music to Huntsville.

229
00:29:38,720 --> 00:29:44,720
It's going to be helping more music get created here, recorded here, developed here and ultimately commercialized from here.

230
00:29:48,720 --> 00:29:53,720
We can also physically see the growth of the ecosystem across the city since the initial audit.

231
00:29:53,720 --> 00:29:58,720
In 2018, we had one hundred and sixty three music related assets mapped throughout the city.

232
00:29:58,720 --> 00:30:01,720
Today we've grown that number to two hundred and fifteen.

233
00:30:01,720 --> 00:30:03,720
So that's a thirty two percent increase.

234
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:14,720
Those assets include venues, recording and rehearsal spaces, music education spots, retail production companies and other businesses and organizations that make the music ecosystem function.

235
00:30:14,720 --> 00:30:18,720
So this isn't simply just a story about having more concerts.

236
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:23,720
It's infrastructure underneath Huntsville's music industry that has grown significantly, too.

237
00:30:25,720 --> 00:30:27,720
Here's one I'm especially proud of.

238
00:30:27,720 --> 00:30:31,720
Huntsville now hosts more than two thousand two hundred music events every year.

239
00:30:31,720 --> 00:30:41,720
That's everything from major nights at the Orion and the Bon Braun Center to local venues, festivals, neighborhood events, songwriter nights and grassroots community performances.

240
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:44,720
And that variety is important and it matters.

241
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:48,720
A strong music city isn't really defined by one venue, one artist or one festival.

242
00:30:48,720 --> 00:30:53,720
It's defined by the depth and diversity of opportunities happening throughout our entire community.

243
00:30:54,720 --> 00:31:00,720
And as part of the San diplomacy work, they did some specific case studies into our partner venues and events.

244
00:31:00,720 --> 00:31:06,720
The Bon Braun Center, as well knows, been the centerpiece of Huntsville's music and entertainment landscape for now more than 50 years.

245
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:10,720
And its economic impact continues to be extraordinary.

246
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:20,720
And twenty twenty five alone, VBC concerts generated approximately thirty seven point five million in economic impact and an estimated five point three million in local tax revenue.

247
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,720
And that activity stands well beyond the walls of the VBC.

248
00:31:23,720 --> 00:31:29,720
It brings people downtown. It fills hotels, restaurants and supports businesses throughout our community.

249
00:31:29,720 --> 00:31:36,720
The VBC remains one of the clearest examples of how music and entertainment infrastructure can generate real economic value for Huntsville.

250
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:42,720
The Orion amphitheater has expanded that impact even further.

251
00:31:43,720 --> 00:31:52,720
In its first 18 months, the Orion welcomed approximately three hundred and fifteen thousand attendees and generated eighty five point two million in economic output.

252
00:31:52,720 --> 00:31:57,720
So nearly one hundred and fifty thousand of those tickets were sold to non-local residents and attendees.

253
00:31:57,720 --> 00:32:06,720
So that's significant because those people are choosing to come to Huntsville because of music and also spending money throughout our community while they're here outside of the amphitheater.

254
00:32:06,720 --> 00:32:11,720
And so that's a very form of a very tangible form of cultural tourism that we're able to prove.

255
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:17,720
The South Star Music Festival demonstrated Huntsville's potential on another scale.

256
00:32:17,720 --> 00:32:26,720
The festival, even though we lost the second day due to the hurricane, it generated an estimated twenty four point one million in economic impact in that single day.

257
00:32:26,720 --> 00:32:34,720
While the national attention surrounding its launch generated approximately two hundred and eighteen million dollars in earned media value.

258
00:32:34,720 --> 00:32:36,720
Those are remarkable numbers.

259
00:32:36,720 --> 00:32:44,720
And when we put that together with the BBC and the Orion, they tell a pretty clear story that Huntsville isn't simply hosting more shows.

260
00:32:44,720 --> 00:32:51,720
Music is creating jobs, it's driving tourism and generating real economic and cultural value for our city.

261
00:32:53,720 --> 00:32:54,720
Industry partnerships.

262
00:32:54,720 --> 00:33:00,720
We are building a sustainable city can only be about bringing great artists and events to Huntsville.

263
00:33:00,720 --> 00:33:05,720
We also have to create pathways for Huntsville artists and music professionals to build careers here.

264
00:33:05,720 --> 00:33:14,720
That's why relationships and partnerships we've established with organizations like the Recording Academy, Universal Music Group and East Irish Studios in Nashville are so important.

265
00:33:14,720 --> 00:33:22,720
They help connect our local creative community to industry expertise, professional networks and opportunities that frankly they don't have here in Huntsville currently.

266
00:33:22,720 --> 00:33:32,720
And they help us build and strengthen the business side infrastructure around our ecosystem needed for that long term sustainable growth to to keep this moving upward.

267
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:37,720
Because ultimately we want Huntsville to be more than just a great place to play and experience music.

268
00:33:37,720 --> 00:33:40,720
We want it to be a great place to build a career in music.

269
00:33:41,720 --> 00:33:43,720
And so what is next?

270
00:33:43,720 --> 00:33:46,720
That brings us to that very important question.

271
00:33:46,720 --> 00:33:49,720
The updated audit gives us another roadmap that we'll be focusing on.

272
00:33:49,720 --> 00:33:58,720
We need to continue investing in the things that have led to our success to get us here, like the artist support, music education, the festival and the venue infrastructure.

273
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:02,720
But we also need to really focus and continue expanding the business side of the industry.

274
00:34:02,720 --> 00:34:16,720
We need more studios, more managers and more agents and just more overall licensing support and other professional services that are needed to help artists sustain those careers here in Huntsville where they don't feel like they need to move to Nashville.

275
00:34:16,720 --> 00:34:20,720
Huntsville has become very, very good at bringing music here.

276
00:34:20,720 --> 00:34:25,720
And our next opportunity is helping more music careers and businesses grow from here.

277
00:34:25,720 --> 00:34:28,720
That doesn't mean moving away from live music by any means.

278
00:34:28,720 --> 00:34:40,720
It just means building on the success we already have so that more of the talents, intellectual property, businesses and economic values surrounding music can be created, developed and retained here in Huntsville.

279
00:34:40,720 --> 00:34:45,720
We've built the live music economy and now we need to build more of the industry around it.

280
00:34:48,720 --> 00:34:51,720
One final piece of our story that I'm especially proud of.

281
00:34:51,720 --> 00:34:54,720
Huntsville is now helping lead this work nationally.

282
00:34:54,720 --> 00:34:58,720
The Huntsville Music Office is a proud founding member of the Association of Music Offices.

283
00:34:58,720 --> 00:35:15,720
That's a recently launched new national network bringing public music offices and industry partners together from across the country to share best practices, strengthen connections between cities and states and work on advancing the role that music can play in economic development, quality of life and community building.

284
00:35:15,720 --> 00:35:22,720
For Huntsville, it's another sign that the work we've started here eight years ago is now part of a much broader national conversation.

285
00:35:22,720 --> 00:35:31,720
And we're at the table alongside music offices and partners from across the country and industry partners like the Recording Academy who produces the Grammy Awards.

286
00:35:31,720 --> 00:35:40,720
That matters because cities and states are increasingly looking at music now not just as entertainment but as part of their broader economic and community development strategy.

287
00:35:40,720 --> 00:35:44,720
And it's exciting because Huntsville isn't just participating in that conversation.

288
00:35:44,720 --> 00:35:47,720
We're actively helping shaping it and we're actively helping lead it.

289
00:35:47,720 --> 00:35:50,720
And I think that says a lot about how far we've come.

290
00:35:50,720 --> 00:35:57,720
Eight years ago we were looking around the country for models and today Huntsville is a model that other cities and communities are looking at.

291
00:35:57,720 --> 00:36:01,720
Huntsville is a very special place.

292
00:36:01,720 --> 00:36:03,720
We all love it here. That's why we're here.

293
00:36:03,720 --> 00:36:16,720
And while the work we're doing is about economic output, jobs, tourism, venues and businesses and investment and all that exciting stuff, it's also about building a city where people can live, create, perform and grow.

294
00:36:16,720 --> 00:36:21,720
One of the most meaningful things we consistently hear from artists is that Huntsville doesn't just show up as an audience.

295
00:36:21,720 --> 00:36:25,720
We show up as a community and artists feel welcomed here.

296
00:36:25,720 --> 00:36:32,720
They feel supported and they see Huntsville as a place where they can not only go on stage but they can build a life and not just play a show.

297
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:37,720
So eight years ago Huntsville made a deliberate decision to treat music as more than just entertainment.

298
00:36:37,720 --> 00:36:46,720
And I just want to say thank you to Mayor Battle, the support of our city council for that process, all our partners and our local music community.

299
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:48,720
And we've made tremendous progress.

300
00:36:48,720 --> 00:36:53,720
And this audit gives us data to show how far we've come and a roadmap on where we're going next.

301
00:36:53,720 --> 00:37:01,720
And so we've built the stage and now it's our opportunity to build even more careers, businesses and create a value around it.

302
00:37:01,720 --> 00:37:03,720
So thank you.

303
00:37:03,720 --> 00:37:09,720
Thank you, Mr. Mandrell and thank you to you and your mighty team of two for everything that you have accomplished together.

304
00:37:09,720 --> 00:37:14,720
Council members, do you have any comments or questions?

305
00:37:14,720 --> 00:37:16,720
Mr. Little. Good job, Matt.

306
00:37:16,720 --> 00:37:18,720
And thanks for a great presentation.

307
00:37:18,720 --> 00:37:21,720
I'm just curious how the grant programs are going.

308
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:24,720
It's going great. It's going really great.

309
00:37:24,720 --> 00:37:26,720
We haven't maxed out our annual 50k yet.

310
00:37:26,720 --> 00:37:29,720
We weren't expecting to, but I think we got close to 40 this year.

311
00:37:29,720 --> 00:37:38,720
And we're seeing it's interesting because we're hearing a lot of stories and testimonials about people that are moving here specifically to take advantage of that program.

312
00:37:38,720 --> 00:37:40,720
And so it's really exciting.

313
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:41,720
Good. Great. Thank you.

314
00:37:41,720 --> 00:37:43,720
Keep up the good work.

315
00:37:43,720 --> 00:37:44,720
Any other comments?

316
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:50,720
Thank you again for the presentation, for the work you're doing and for demonstrating impact in our community.

317
00:37:50,720 --> 00:37:56,720
Thank you.

318
00:37:56,720 --> 00:37:59,720
We have no matters with outside legal representative.

319
00:37:59,720 --> 00:38:01,720
We will move on to public hearings to be held.

320
00:38:01,720 --> 00:38:12,720
Nine A is a public hearing authorizing community development to assess the cost of cutting overgrown grass and weeds against certain properties and consideration of a resolution pertaining to the same.

321
00:38:12,720 --> 00:38:13,720
Mr. Erwin.

322
00:38:13,720 --> 00:38:16,720
Good evening, Council Member Sparrow and the Community Development Department.

323
00:38:16,720 --> 00:38:23,720
This is a public hearing on a resolution authorizing community development to assess the cost for cutting overgrown grass and weeds at certain properties.

324
00:38:23,720 --> 00:38:30,720
The resolution before you authorizes assessments in the amount of $10,937.59.

325
00:38:30,720 --> 00:38:40,720
The resolution covers 36 total properties, 21 head owners inside the city limits of Huntsville, six were out of the city limits of Huntsville with nine out of state owners.

326
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:41,720
Thank you.

327
00:38:41,720 --> 00:38:43,720
The public hearing is now open.

328
00:38:43,720 --> 00:38:47,720
Is there anyone who wishes to comment on this item?

329
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:50,720
Please come to the microphone here, sir.

330
00:38:50,720 --> 00:38:53,720
If you will give us your name and address for the record.

331
00:38:53,720 --> 00:38:58,720
Hello, my name is Mohammed Arunajad.

332
00:38:58,720 --> 00:39:04,720
I'm the owner of property at 1330 Chikamaga Trail.

333
00:39:04,720 --> 00:39:09,720
I received a notice from the Department of Community Development.

334
00:39:09,720 --> 00:39:16,720
It said I had some like three weeks to get more property shaped up because tenants had moved out.

335
00:39:16,720 --> 00:39:23,720
